in this course I hope we can capture  some of Walt Whitman's enthusiasm for  the strong light works of Engineers so  we explore the great structures of every  era from an engineering perspective  let's begin our journey with a visit to  a structure that's indisputably one of  the world's greatest the Brooklyn Bridge  when we look at a magnificent work like  this one we see three essential  qualities form function and structure  perhaps the most obvious of these three  is function how people use the bridge  function of the Brooklyn Bridge is to  span the East River linking Brooklyn and  Manhattan originally for pedestrians  horse-drawn vehicles and trolleys but  today for six lanes of automobile  traffic now back in the 1800s that  function was pretty significant and the  economic and social impacts of linking  those two cities was really quite  important today however it's really not  the function but the form that  stimulates our emotions and captures our  imagination  so what's form form refers to as  aesthetic appeal the physical features  of a building bridge are tower-like  shape scale proportion ornament and the  form of the Brooklyn Bridge certainly is  beautiful  the graceful curve of those main cables  the strength and solidity of the granite  towers that intricate web of steel  framework that supports the roadway we  think of these features as being  primarily aesthetic but there's so much  more they are in fact a direct  reflection of the scientific principles  underlying the design of this Great  Bridge they are in a word structure a  system of low carrying elements that  causes the bridge to stand up now to  illustrate this point I'd like to take a  look at a model of a typical suspension  bridge you see here this model consists  of two main cables the cables are  anchored out here on the ends they  extend up over the tops of these two  tall towers and then they're draped  across the main span in that  characteristic curved shape from the  cables you see that I have suspend  did a series of weights there equal  weights and they're all spaced an equal  distance apart so this constitutes a  uniform loading on the cable the shape  of the cable then is a direct  consequence of that loading it's really  a function of physics the shape of the  cable mathematically speaking is a  parabola and the parabola occurs  specifically as a consequence of that  loading it's science not aesthetics I'll  pick the tops of the towers the cable  makes a sharp bend and as a direct  consequence of that Bend all of the  tension in the cable is transmitted  downward into the towers and so in  effect the two towers are carrying the  entire weight of the bridge and  everything that's that's applied to that  bridge in compression directly down  through the towers and that's why on the  Brooklyn Bridge the towers are so  massive there's such robust granite  structures so the parabolic shape of the  curve of the cable is caused by the  uniform loading what happens if the  loading isn't uniform what happens for  example if a vehicle a heavy vehicle  like a train or a heavy truck is  crossing the span well when that heavy  vehicle gets out here to mid span no  what happens  note that the parabolic shape of the  cables has now become distorted into  something very different one of the  characteristic features of a cable  structure is that it changes the shape  in response to changes in applied  loading well that's all well and good  but if this is a functional suspension  bridge we have a problem because that  sort of distortion in the cable also  caused a significant downward deflection  of the deck that the vehicles are  driving across so imagine if you're the  occupant or driver of a vehicle that's  moving from one side of the bridge to  the other when the vehicle is located  here the cable distorts into this shape  as it moves to this location the cable  distorts again but into a different  shape by the time it gets out to mid  span it's distorted into yet another  shape again and all the while the bridge  is undulating and undergoing all these  crazy deformations clearly it's not  practical as a functional structure and  that's why most suspension bridges to  include the Brooklyn Bridge use a  secondary structure called a stiff  Trust which is supported directly from  the cable and I do want to emphasize  that the stiffening trust is not resting  on the towers note that there's a space  between the ends of the truss and the  towers it's entirely hanging from the  cable but now when I take this same  heavy concentrated load of the vehicle  crossing the bridge and we place it on  the stiffening truss we see that the  shape of the cables didn't change at all  but the stiffening trust does is to  distribute that heavy load out laterally  so that all of these slender vertical  wires they're called suspenders carry  that load instead of just one or two  suspenders as you saw in the case of the  unn stiffened deck so what we see is  that all of these prominent aspects of  the form of the Brooklyn Bridge the  shape of the cables the robust stone  towers the stiffening trusses they all  derive from structural purpose as this  demonstration shows we really can't  fully appreciate and understand the  Brooklyn Bridge based on its form and  its function alone we need to see and  understand its structure as what
 

